tle"} "Bantamweight, Greco-Roman (≤58 kg), Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "6 – 10 July 1924"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/266"} "Vélodrome d'Hiver, Paris"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/121216"} "25 from 15 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Double-elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the first time a class below 60 kg was held at the Olympics in Greco-Roman. At the 1922 World Championships, Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/60127"} "Fritiof Svensson"] " had won, with Estonian " [:a {:href "/athletes/57829"} "Eduard Pütsep"] " second. However, Svensson moved up in weight and competed in Paris as a " [:a {:href "/results/121268"} "featherweight"] ", leaving the favorite role to Pütsep. He did not disappoint, winning all his matches to take the gold medal, with his victory in round four over Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/57862"} "Anselm Ahlfors"] " being decisive. Ahlfors took silver, with the bronze going to his countryman " [:a {:href "/athletes/57890"} "Väinö Ikonen"] ", who had been the 1921 World Champion in this class. Pütsep would later win a silver medal at the 1927 European Championships, and compete at the 1928 Olympics, but with no medals. Ahlfors never competed at a World or European Championship, but won the 1925 Nordic Championships in this event."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/57829"} "Eduard Pütsep"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EST"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/EST.png"}] "EST"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]]